# Tests
# Excluded, and the reason is worth writing down because the opposite is
# arguable. Measured on this tree with scripts/deploy_bundle.py's own matcher,
# immediately before this rule was added: tests/ was 114 of the 259 shipped files
# and 1,657,238 of the 3,439,447 shipped bytes -- 44.0% of the files and 48.2% of
# the upload, for code Vercel never executes. After it, 145 files and 1,782,228
# bytes.
#
# The argument for shipping it is "the uploaded artifact is verifiable": you
# could run the suite against exactly what was uploaded. That ability does not
# exist today in any form. Vercel does not publish the artifact, no script in
# this repo fetches one, and .github/workflows/deploy-bundle.yml runs the bundle
# check with `--tree .` -- the working tree, not a downloaded upload. So keeping
# tests/ buys a capability nobody has, at half the upload.
#
# It also costs the check its strongest property. With tests/ shipped, a shipped
# module that wrongly imported something under tests/ would import cleanly, and
# the cross-boundary check could not see the edge. tests/test_deploy_bundle.py
# pins the decision so the next person does not flip it by accident.
tests/
pytest/
.coverage
htmlcov/
.tox/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Documentation
*.md
!README.md

# Scripts
scripts/
*.sh

# Source code not needed for UI
src/ingestion/
src/utils/
src/verification/*
src/celery/
src/worker/
# Keep src/shared, src/schema and the UI-imported verification module
!src/shared/
!src/schema/
!src/verification/__init__.py
!src/verification/event_identity.py

# Node (if any)
node_modules/

# Vercel-specific exclusions
# uv.lock is excluded because Vercel resolves from requirements.txt; CI resolves
# from the lock. Anything in the lock and not in requirements.txt therefore passes
# CI and fails the deploy, which is what the uv.lock line of the check reports.
uv.lock
docs/
